const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"RC_E1_001","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P1","passage":"In measuring ageism's impact, researchers have found that it shortens lives and costs billions. It also seeps into the culture where it's magnified by our institutions. Our personal choices about aging aren't made in a vacuum. They're made in the context of the culture and the options available to us. So, improving how we age in our own lives means pushing our systems to improve, too.\\n\\nToo often we think of old age as an island when in actuality, we age not only as individuals but also as parts of families and communities, its repercussions rippling through both. And although we tend to freeze \\"old\\" in time, how we age is entwined with what happens earlier in life. Unaddressed problems build over the years. By late life, the snowball has become an avalanche. For well-being in aging, we need to better support people—caregivers and care recipients—through the whole life cycle.\\n\\nThis story of American aging is also shaped by another American story: our preoccupation with independence. By elevating adamant self-sufficiency to a singular virtue, we set traps that ensnare us in isolation and loneliness despite ample evidence that it's in fact interdependence that leads to better health and well-being.\\n\\nAnother cultural inclination that serves us poorly is that we tend to wait until trouble happens and then react, instead of trying to get out ahead of it. Identifying the kinds of early and midcourse adjustments that might prevent trouble could do much to enhance our long lives.\\n\\nUnlikely as it sounds, I found an illuminating model in automobile safety. When car companies started installing seatbelts, auto fatalities dropped. But medical examiners who investigated car crash injuries found patterns revealing that not all seatbelts were equal. Forensic analysis made the invisible more visible. Lap belts were better than no belt, but they didn't protect upper bodies from catapulting into windshields and steering wheels. Such observations, and research confirming them, changed emergency medicine, car manufacturing, consumer safety practices, laws, and eventually social norms, preventing millions of traffic fatalities.\\n\\nReducing harm required looking closely at the grim aftermath and analyzing what went wrong, not just once, but many, many times to reveal the patterns. I wanted to trace the problems with aging I encountered back to their sources, try to discern the patterns, and better understand what progress might look like.\\n\\nAging plunges all of us into new worlds, each one with its own language and ways. Families often split up its diverse demands—care, finances, errands, insurance—depending on who's closest or best suited for what. No one can know or do it all. A similar dividing up of responsibility happened in my professional life. The translators and guides who helped me understand the many worlds of aging and how these worlds intersect—among them a doctor, a criminal prosecutor, a social worker, an elder lawyer, and a banker—are characters in this book.\\n\\nIn writing about people and systems, I inhabited a liminal role—simultaneously participant and observer, colleague, and chronicler. I tried to write what I knew, but as I looked more closely, instead of finding clear answers, often I found deep questions about the meanings of autonomy and safety, of compassion and justice. I learned how the trade-offs between them can evolve over a life, and how those trade-offs differ by person, family, and culture. What I learned changed me, too. For one thing, I started out as a prosecutor and emerged a \\"preventionist.\\" This book tells that story, as well.","question":"All of the following can be inferred from the passage, EXCEPT:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"The book that is referred to in the passage talks about the journey of the author discovering ageism and its implications on one's life and the surroundings they delve into.","B":"Ageing process is exclusively a personal experience that includes families, communities, or social structures.","C":"Understanding the intricacies of justice, safety, autonomy, and compassion brings up more profound issues related to ageing.","D":"Similar to automobile safety, aging is something that can be planned by understanding the threats that develop in the future."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Understanding the intricacies of justice, safety, autonomy, and compassion brings up more profound issues related to ageing.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"Option A can be inferred from the passage as it is rightly mentioned in the penultimate paragraph that they are characters in the book and that he/she delves into the journey of her experience with ageism. Option B can also be inferred from the penultimate paragraph. Option D can be inferred from the fifth paragraph. Hence, A, B and D are eliminated. This leaves us with Option C which cannot be inferred from the passage as it reverses the understanding we get from the last paragraph by claiming the vice versa of what is mentioned.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E1_002","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P1","passage":"In measuring ageism's impact, researchers have found that it shortens lives and costs billions. It also seeps into the culture where it's magnified by our institutions. Our personal choices about aging aren't made in a vacuum. They're made in the context of the culture and the options available to us. So, improving how we age in our own lives means pushing our systems to improve, too.\\n\\nToo often we think of old age as an island when in actuality, we age not only as individuals but also as parts of families and communities, its repercussions rippling through both. And although we tend to freeze \\"old\\" in time, how we age is entwined with what happens earlier in life. Unaddressed problems build over the years. By late life, the snowball has become an avalanche. For well-being in aging, we need to better support people—caregivers and care recipients—through the whole life cycle.\\n\\nThis story of American aging is also shaped by another American story: our preoccupation with independence. By elevating adamant self-sufficiency to a singular virtue, we set traps that ensnare us in isolation and loneliness despite ample evidence that it's in fact interdependence that leads to better health and well-being.\\n\\nAnother cultural inclination that serves us poorly is that we tend to wait until trouble happens and then react, instead of trying to get out ahead of it. Identifying the kinds of early and midcourse adjustments that might prevent trouble could do much to enhance our long lives.\\n\\nUnlikely as it sounds, I found an illuminating model in automobile safety. When car companies started installing seatbelts, auto fatalities dropped. But medical examiners who investigated car crash injuries found patterns revealing that not all seatbelts were equal. Forensic analysis made the invisible more visible. Lap belts were better than no belt, but they didn't protect upper bodies from catapulting into windshields and steering wheels. Such observations, and research confirming them, changed emergency medicine, car manufacturing, consumer safety practices, laws, and eventually social norms, preventing millions of traffic fatalities.\\n\\nReducing harm required looking closely at the grim aftermath and analyzing what went wrong, not just once, but many, many times to reveal the patterns. I wanted to trace the problems with aging I encountered back to their sources, try to discern the patterns, and better understand what progress might look like.\\n\\nAging plunges all of us into new worlds, each one with its own language and ways. Families often split up its diverse demands—care, finances, errands, insurance—depending on who's closest or best suited for what. No one can know or do it all. A similar dividing up of responsibility happened in my professional life. The translators and guides who helped me understand the many worlds of aging and how these worlds intersect—among them a doctor, a criminal prosecutor, a social worker, an elder lawyer, and a banker—are characters in this book.\\n\\nIn writing about people and systems, I inhabited a liminal role—simultaneously participant and observer, colleague, and chronicler. I tried to write what I knew, but as I looked more closely, instead of finding clear answers, often I found deep questions about the meanings of autonomy and safety, of compassion and justice. I learned how the trade-offs between them can evolve over a life, and how those trade-offs differ by person, family, and culture. What I learned changed me, too. For one thing, I started out as a prosecutor and emerged a \\"preventionist.\\" This book tells that story, as well.","question":"All of the following statements, if true, would strengthen the claim that ageism needs to be managed from early on EXCEPT:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Countries with aging-friendly policies like Japan, where intergenerational living is encouraged, are studied to enhance the effectiveness of policies here in the US.","B":"People have experienced age discrimination in healthcare and work environments on several occasions, regardless of economic and social status.","C":"Economic studies show positive correlation between social support networks and healthy aging.","D":"Since they are aware that they are forcing themselves on others, elders are often open to new situations and adjustments."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Since they are aware that they are forcing themselves on others, elders are often open to new situations and adjustments.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption","B":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption","C":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption"},"explanation":"Option A talks about getting inspired by countries like Japan to strengthen the policies in the US which is in support of the claim that ageism needs to be managed. Option B highlights the discrimination elderly face, which can be inferred as a reason for the need for management of ageism. Option C too strengthens the claim as it shows that social support networks that help manage aging lead to healthy aging. While Option D talks about elders being open to new situations and adjustments, it does not address the need for early management of ageism, making it the correct answer.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E1_003","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P1","passage":"In measuring ageism's impact, researchers have found that it shortens lives and costs billions. It also seeps into the culture where it's magnified by our institutions. Our personal choices about aging aren't made in a vacuum. They're made in the context of the culture and the options available to us. So, improving how we age in our own lives means pushing our systems to improve, too.\\n\\nToo often we think of old age as an island when in actuality, we age not only as individuals but also as parts of families and communities, its repercussions rippling through both. And although we tend to freeze \\"old\\" in time, how we age is entwined with what happens earlier in life. Unaddressed problems build over the years. By late life, the snowball has become an avalanche. For well-being in aging, we need to better support people—caregivers and care recipients—through the whole life cycle.\\n\\nThis story of American aging is also shaped by another American story: our preoccupation with independence. By elevating adamant self-sufficiency to a singular virtue, we set traps that ensnare us in isolation and loneliness despite ample evidence that it's in fact interdependence that leads to better health and well-being.\\n\\nAnother cultural inclination that serves us poorly is that we tend to wait until trouble happens and then react, instead of trying to get out ahead of it. Identifying the kinds of early and midcourse adjustments that might prevent trouble could do much to enhance our long lives.\\n\\nUnlikely as it sounds, I found an illuminating model in automobile safety. When car companies started installing seatbelts, auto fatalities dropped. But medical examiners who investigated car crash injuries found patterns revealing that not all seatbelts were equal. Forensic analysis made the invisible more visible. Lap belts were better than no belt, but they didn't protect upper bodies from catapulting into windshields and steering wheels. Such observations, and research confirming them, changed emergency medicine, car manufacturing, consumer safety practices, laws, and eventually social norms, preventing millions of traffic fatalities.\\n\\nReducing harm required looking closely at the grim aftermath and analyzing what went wrong, not just once, but many, many times to reveal the patterns. I wanted to trace the problems with aging I encountered back to their sources, try to discern the patterns, and better understand what progress might look like.\\n\\nAging plunges all of us into new worlds, each one with its own language and ways. Families often split up its diverse demands—care, finances, errands, insurance—depending on who's closest or best suited for what. No one can know or do it all. A similar dividing up of responsibility happened in my professional life. The translators and guides who helped me understand the many worlds of aging and how these worlds intersect—among them a doctor, a criminal prosecutor, a social worker, an elder lawyer, and a banker—are characters in this book.\\n\\nIn writing about people and systems, I inhabited a liminal role—simultaneously participant and observer, colleague, and chronicler. I tried to write what I knew, but as I looked more closely, instead of finding clear answers, often I found deep questions about the meanings of autonomy and safety, of compassion and justice. I learned how the trade-offs between them can evolve over a life, and how those trade-offs differ by person, family, and culture. What I learned changed me, too. For one thing, I started out as a prosecutor and emerged a \\"preventionist.\\" This book tells that story, as well.","question":"Which of the following is not an example of \\"the snowball has become an avalanche\\"?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"Ignoring warning signs of financial trouble resulted in mounting debts and bankruptcy.","B":"A pile of clothes mounting on a chair kept getting more loaded until it all crashed down on the floor.","C":"The lies told piled up so much so that it became a whole scheme of deceit by the time the liar realised it.","D":"Avoiding confrontation allowed resentment to fester, erupting into a heated argument."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"A pile of clothes mounting on a chair kept getting more loaded until it all crashed down on the floor.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption","C":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption","D":"confuses_weakener_with_assumption"},"explanation":"Option B does not illustrate the concept of 'the snowball has become an avalanche' as described in the passage. Instead, it describes a situation of accumulating clothes falling down, which is not analogous to the gradual buildup of unaddressed problems leading to significant consequences. The metaphor in the passage refers to unaddressed problems compounding over time into a crisis — A, C, and D all capture this pattern of neglected issues escalating into major consequences.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E1_004","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P2","passage":"The first time I took a calligraphy course was about a year ago, and the decision was quite hard. I was sure that it would be painstaking and that I would need excellent handwriting to learn the art. How mistaken I was!\\n\\nBut, I have better understood what calligraphy is by understanding what it is not. It is not just about decorated text, nor is it only about the use of letters as ornaments. True, calligraphic letters and pages are very often dressed up with some form of decoration; gold colour, pattern, pictures, flourishes etc. but this ornamental layer only decorates the underlying FORM. There is a big difference between a beautiful form and a beautiful ornament on the top of the form. Calligraphy is about symbols themselves being beautifully formed and arranged. Naked, undecorated calligraphy should look good just as it is, in the same way that athletes, models, and healthy people would look good without their clothes on.\\n\\nIs calligraphy beautiful handwriting? Not exactly and not entirely. It is more than just that. It is true that the derivation of the word translates simply as 'beautiful writing'. But the word has taken on a larger meaning. Bear in mind that the first goals of handwriting are to be quickly and easily written and accurately read. Beauty, personality and artistic impact are not as important in handwriting as are clarity and speed. So, although calligraphy is a kind of handwriting, and some handwriting is calligraphic in appearance, they are not the same. It is the difference between 'writing as an art form' and 'artistic-looking handwriting'. Calligraphy aims to produce an 'art' reaction, in which a deeper meaning is communicated from artist to viewer, and the viewer feels invited to think a new thought in response. Handwriting, by contrast, aims to be read.","question":"The central idea of the passage is that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"writing as an art form attains the level of calligraphy only with proper decoration.","B":"the art of calligraphy lies in clarity, even without much or any decoration.","C":"calligraphy has not remained a popular art form because of its intricacies of decoration and clarity.","D":"calligraphy is a tangential art form because its decoration has to gain precedence over clarity."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"the art of calligraphy lies in clarity, even without much or any decoration.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","C":"out_of_scope","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"The first paragraph introduces the art of calligraphy, the second paragraph posits that decoration is secondary and the final paragraph indicates that clarity is more important than 'artistic nature' of writing. Choice A is incorrect because the author does not say that 'decoration' is an essential element of calligraphy. Choice B is CORRECT as per the reasons given above. Choice C is incorrect because the popularity of calligraphy has not been commented on. Choice D is incorrect because the author's tone towards calligraphy is not negative.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E1_005","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P2","passage":"The first time I took a calligraphy course was about a year ago, and the decision was quite hard. I was sure that it would be painstaking and that I would need excellent handwriting to learn the art. How mistaken I was!\\n\\nBut, I have better understood what calligraphy is by understanding what it is not. It is not just about decorated text, nor is it only about the use of letters as ornaments. True, calligraphic letters and pages are very often dressed up with some form of decoration; gold colour, pattern, pictures, flourishes etc. but this ornamental layer only decorates the underlying FORM. There is a big difference between a beautiful form and a beautiful ornament on the top of the form. Calligraphy is about symbols themselves being beautifully formed and arranged. Naked, undecorated calligraphy should look good just as it is, in the same way that athletes, models, and healthy people would look good without their clothes on.\\n\\nIs calligraphy beautiful handwriting? Not exactly and not entirely. It is more than just that. It is true that the derivation of the word translates simply as 'beautiful writing'. But the word has taken on a larger meaning. Bear in mind that the first goals of handwriting are to be quickly and easily written and accurately read. Beauty, personality and artistic impact are not as important in handwriting as are clarity and speed. So, although calligraphy is a kind of handwriting, and some handwriting is calligraphic in appearance, they are not the same. It is the difference between 'writing as an art form' and 'artistic-looking handwriting'. Calligraphy aims to produce an 'art' reaction, in which a deeper meaning is communicated from artist to viewer, and the viewer feels invited to think a new thought in response. Handwriting, by contrast, aims to be read.","question":"The author mentions 'athletes, models, and healthy people' to indicate that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"calligraphy uses symbols that are not generally used even in beautiful handwriting.","B":"nakedness without any ornamentation is a form of calligraphy.","C":"the art of calligraphy is not so much an ornamentation of letter as it is beautiful rendering of letters.","D":"use of ornamentation spoils the clarity, the very aim of handwriting."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"the art of calligraphy is not so much an ornamentation of letter as it is beautiful rendering of letters.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","B":"too_extreme","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"In the last two sentences of the second paragraph, the author indicates that form is more important than decoration. Choice A is incorrect because the word 'symbols' here implies whatever we use in writing. Choice B is incorrect because nakedness is not given as a form of calligraphy. Choice C is CORRECT as per the reasons given. Choice D is incorrect because the concept of ornamentation marring clarity is not implied in the passage.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E1_006","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P2","passage":"The first time I took a calligraphy course was about a year ago, and the decision was quite hard. I was sure that it would be painstaking and that I would need excellent handwriting to learn the art. How mistaken I was!\\n\\nBut, I have better understood what calligraphy is by understanding what it is not. It is not just about decorated text, nor is it only about the use of letters as ornaments. True, calligraphic letters and pages are very often dressed up with some form of decoration; gold colour, pattern, pictures, flourishes etc. but this ornamental layer only decorates the underlying FORM. There is a big difference between a beautiful form and a beautiful ornament on the top of the form. Calligraphy is about symbols themselves being beautifully formed and arranged. Naked, undecorated calligraphy should look good just as it is, in the same way that athletes, models, and healthy people would look good without their clothes on.\\n\\nIs calligraphy beautiful handwriting? Not exactly and not entirely. It is more than just that. It is true that the derivation of the word translates simply as 'beautiful writing'. But the word has taken on a larger meaning. Bear in mind that the first goals of handwriting are to be quickly and easily written and accurately read. Beauty, personality and artistic impact are not as important in handwriting as are clarity and speed. So, although calligraphy is a kind of handwriting, and some handwriting is calligraphic in appearance, they are not the same. It is the difference between 'writing as an art form' and 'artistic-looking handwriting'. Calligraphy aims to produce an 'art' reaction, in which a deeper meaning is communicated from artist to viewer, and the viewer feels invited to think a new thought in response. Handwriting, by contrast, aims to be read.","question":"The author's conclusions about calligraphy are based on the assumption that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"the more ornamental one's writing is, the more calligraphic his writing proves to be.","B":"good form in writing facilitates clarity of handwriting, making reading a pleasant experience.","C":"calligraphy is an art form that should be encouraged despite the painstaking nature of the art.","D":"calligraphy is just one facet of handwriting that has clarity and ornamentation."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"good form in writing facilitates clarity of handwriting, making reading a pleasant experience.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Overall, the author concludes that calligraphy includes good form with proper ornamentation and should result in clarity of writing. We must identify the assumption that supports this conclusion. Choice A is incorrect because the author does not correlate the degree of ornamentation and the degree of calligraphy. Choice B is CORRECT because if this is true, the author's conclusions hold good. Choice C is incorrect because the author does not posit, in the first place, that it is difficult. Choice D is incorrect because of the tone of the option.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E1_007","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P3","passage":"There are those who believe that philosophy of religion was made pointless by Hume and Kant. These two thinkers are widely thought to have driven the final nail into the coffin of rational theology and to have shown not only that all previous attempts to prove the existence of God had failed, but that all future attempts were bound to suffer the same fate.\\n\\nNot all contemporary philosophers, of course, accept that Kant and Hume succeeded in refuting such arguments. Some thinkers have no particular regard for the eighteenth-century Enlightenment, in any case: Thomism is still alive, respected in certain quarters, and quietly confident of the ultimate intellectual bankruptcy of secular, atheistic philosophies. Furthermore, Kant was, in fact, a pious Christian, and it is not certain that even Hume was an atheist, though it is quite likely. Nevertheless, since the eighteenth century, rational theology has lost much of its confidence.\\n\\nBut nowadays, even among many who wish that philosophy could do more for religion, there is an air of quite resignation and a reliance upon confused and feeble platitudes. This atmosphere contains the oxygen for anti-religious triumphalism, as manifested in particular by the likes of Dr. Richard Dawkins, the Oxford zoologist and freelance evangelizing atheist. Those like Dawkins – who often resort to arguments which are superficial and philosophically naive – are made to seem victorious by the lack of confidence and intellectual confusion of some of those who speak for orthodox religion.","question":"The author of the passage can overall be considered a supporter of","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"eighteenth century Enlightenment.","B":"rational theology.","C":"orthodox religion.","D":"anti-religious triumphalism."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"rational theology.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","C":"correct_but_unstated","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"The author starts the passage with the refutation of rational theology by Kant and Hume and goes on to tell that their arguments are not acceptable. Thus, he is likely to be a supporter of rational theology. Choice A is incorrect because in the first two sentences of the second paragraph, we can infer that Hume and Kant were supporters of Enlightenment and the author criticizes them. Choice B is CORRECT as per the reasons given. Choice C is incorrect because the author is likely to support religion, but information present in the passage does not substantiate that he supports orthodox religion specifically. Choice D is incorrect because the author does not support anti-religious ideas.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E1_008","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P3","passage":"There are those who believe that philosophy of religion was made pointless by Hume and Kant. These two thinkers are widely thought to have driven the final nail into the coffin of rational theology and to have shown not only that all previous attempts to prove the existence of God had failed, but that all future attempts were bound to suffer the same fate.\\n\\nNot all contemporary philosophers, of course, accept that Kant and Hume succeeded in refuting such arguments. Some thinkers have no particular regard for the eighteenth-century Enlightenment, in any case: Thomism is still alive, respected in certain quarters, and quietly confident of the ultimate intellectual bankruptcy of secular, atheistic philosophies. Furthermore, Kant was, in fact, a pious Christian, and it is not certain that even Hume was an atheist, though it is quite likely. Nevertheless, since the eighteenth century, rational theology has lost much of its confidence.\\n\\nBut nowadays, even among many who wish that philosophy could do more for religion, there is an air of quite resignation and a reliance upon confused and feeble platitudes. This atmosphere contains the oxygen for anti-religious triumphalism, as manifested in particular by the likes of Dr. Richard Dawkins, the Oxford zoologist and freelance evangelizing atheist. Those like Dawkins – who often resort to arguments which are superficial and philosophically naive – are made to seem victorious by the lack of confidence and intellectual confusion of some of those who speak for orthodox religion.","question":"The author mentions Dawkins in the last paragraph to support the view that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"the resignation of supporters of religion has made them depend on feeble platitudes.","B":"the atmosphere of strong rational theology has made it possible for the likes of Dawkins.","C":"the weakness in philosophical justifications of religion has made anti-religious propaganda successful.","D":"the anti-religious arguments are superficial and philosophically naive."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"the weakness in philosophical justifications of religion has made anti-religious propaganda successful.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"correct_but_unstated","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"In the third paragraph, the author states that philosophers are not able to support religion with strong argument and so people such as Dawkins have succeeded in presenting anti-religious views. To illustrate this point, the author gives the example of Dawkins. Choice A is incorrect because we cannot infer that the said resignation is the reason for the platitudes, and because the author does not mention Dawkins in this context. Choice B is incorrect because it is weak rational theology that has made it possible for the likes of Dawkins. Choice C is CORRECT as per the reasons given above. Choice D is incorrect because, though this statement is true, the author's main purpose of mentioning Dawkins is not to prove this point.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E1_009","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P3","passage":"There are those who believe that philosophy of religion was made pointless by Hume and Kant. These two thinkers are widely thought to have driven the final nail into the coffin of rational theology and to have shown not only that all previous attempts to prove the existence of God had failed, but that all future attempts were bound to suffer the same fate.\\n\\nNot all contemporary philosophers, of course, accept that Kant and Hume succeeded in refuting such arguments. Some thinkers have no particular regard for the eighteenth-century Enlightenment, in any case: Thomism is still alive, respected in certain quarters, and quietly confident of the ultimate intellectual bankruptcy of secular, atheistic philosophies. Furthermore, Kant was, in fact, a pious Christian, and it is not certain that even Hume was an atheist, though it is quite likely. Nevertheless, since the eighteenth century, rational theology has lost much of its confidence.\\n\\nBut nowadays, even among many who wish that philosophy could do more for religion, there is an air of quite resignation and a reliance upon confused and feeble platitudes. This atmosphere contains the oxygen for anti-religious triumphalism, as manifested in particular by the likes of Dr. Richard Dawkins, the Oxford zoologist and freelance evangelizing atheist. Those like Dawkins – who often resort to arguments which are superficial and philosophically naive – are made to seem victorious by the lack of confidence and intellectual confusion of some of those who speak for orthodox religion.","question":"The author's criticism of Kant on the grounds that he is himself a pious Christian is logically questionable because","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"the author is attacking a personal trait of Kant, instead of attacking his argument against rational philosophy.","B":"the author assumes that one who posits a view cannot hold a view that is quite contrary to that view.","C":"the author attacks Kant because the author himself believes in rational theology.","D":"the author's opinion and criticism are outdated because they do not comply with the current standards of critique."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"the author is attacking a personal trait of Kant, instead of attacking his argument against rational philosophy.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"confuses_inference_with_assumption","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"Kant opposes rational theology, but he himself is a pious Christian. The author points this out to question the philosophy of Kant. This implies that the author is questioning Kant's personal trait that goes against his philosophy. Choice A is CORRECT as per the reasons given above. Choice B is incorrect because if the author assumes this, then the author should not attack Kant in the first place. Choice C is incorrect because the instance of criticism is not due to personal bias. Choice D is incorrect because the question of current standards is not relevant here.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E1_010","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P4","passage":"There are several factors that explain the increase in store variety and quality in American grocery stores: the generalized increase in wealth has given households the means to purchase more and better products and created larger markets for more specialized products. The gains in technology and food science, along with the widespread reduction in transaction and transportation costs, have enabled producers to increase quality, get better information on what consumers want, and ship those products to stores more quickly, more safely, and all year round. We take for granted the ability to get almost any fruit or vegetable year-round these days, when that was not possible even as recently as the 1970s or '80s. Some of that is due to food science enabling us to grow things indoors, and ship them more cheaply from far away, but it's also due to the growth of global trade agreements reducing the transaction costs of getting produce where it is in season.\\n\\nSimilar factors are at work in explaining the increase in the variety of grocery stores, as more Americans can afford higher end produce and meats and falling costs have made it possible to supply them. They might wish to get their basics at the warehouse or delivered by Amazon, as quality differences are fewer. This fragmentation of the grocery market parallels that of other markets (e.g., recorded music) as technology and falling transaction costs make thinner markets more profitable and, thereby, enable people to obtain more precise matches for their preferences.","question":"Which of the following best describes what the passage is trying to do?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"It evaluates a business practice followed by a specific business sector.","B":"It analyzes factors responsible for a phenomenon.","C":"It compares different strategies followed by a business sector.","D":"It criticizes a business for making unwise decisions."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"It analyzes factors responsible for a phenomenon.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The passage explains various factors that influenced the observed increase in variety and quality in American grocery stores. The increase being a phenomenon, Option B, which states this, is the best option. Choice A is incorrect because the passage does not talk about any specific business practice. Choice C is incorrect because the passage does not compare any business strategies. Choice D is incorrect because the passage does not point out any unwise business decisions.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E1_011","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P4","passage":"There are several factors that explain the increase in store variety and quality in American grocery stores: the generalized increase in wealth has given households the means to purchase more and better products and created larger markets for more specialized products. The gains in technology and food science, along with the widespread reduction in transaction and transportation costs, have enabled producers to increase quality, get better information on what consumers want, and ship those products to stores more quickly, more safely, and all year round. We take for granted the ability to get almost any fruit or vegetable year-round these days, when that was not possible even as recently as the 1970s or '80s. Some of that is due to food science enabling us to grow things indoors, and ship them more cheaply from far away, but it's also due to the growth of global trade agreements reducing the transaction costs of getting produce where it is in season.\\n\\nSimilar factors are at work in explaining the increase in the variety of grocery stores, as more Americans can afford higher end produce and meats and falling costs have made it possible to supply them. They might wish to get their basics at the warehouse or delivered by Amazon, as quality differences are fewer. This fragmentation of the grocery market parallels that of other markets (e.g., recorded music) as technology and falling transaction costs make thinner markets more profitable and, thereby, enable people to obtain more precise matches for their preferences.","question":"All of the following explain the advancement of grocery business in America EXCEPT that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"gains in technology and food science reduced the input costs.","B":"economic advancement of population has increased the business opportunity.","C":"thinner markets have made the business more competitive.","D":"businesses can have insights about customer preferences, because of technology."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"thinner markets have made the business more competitive.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","B":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"For this EXCEPT question, we need to verify every option individually and eliminate options that actually facilitated the growth of grocery stores. Choice A is incorrect because this has been given as a factor facilitating the growth of grocery stores. Choice B is incorrect because this is a factor as per the first two sentences of the passage. Choice C is CORRECT because this is not a factor facilitating growth; in fact, if markets become thinner and more competitive, profit margins are likely to come down, which adversely affects the growth of grocery stores. Choice D is incorrect because this is given in the second sentence of the first paragraph as a factor.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E1_012","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P4","passage":"There are several factors that explain the increase in store variety and quality in American grocery stores: the generalized increase in wealth has given households the means to purchase more and better products and created larger markets for more specialized products. The gains in technology and food science, along with the widespread reduction in transaction and transportation costs, have enabled producers to increase quality, get better information on what consumers want, and ship those products to stores more quickly, more safely, and all year round. We take for granted the ability to get almost any fruit or vegetable year-round these days, when that was not possible even as recently as the 1970s or '80s. Some of that is due to food science enabling us to grow things indoors, and ship them more cheaply from far away, but it's also due to the growth of global trade agreements reducing the transaction costs of getting produce where it is in season.\\n\\nSimilar factors are at work in explaining the increase in the variety of grocery stores, as more Americans can afford higher end produce and meats and falling costs have made it possible to supply them. They might wish to get their basics at the warehouse or delivered by Amazon, as quality differences are fewer. This fragmentation of the grocery market parallels that of other markets (e.g., recorded music) as technology and falling transaction costs make thinner markets more profitable and, thereby, enable people to obtain more precise matches for their preferences.","question":"Which of the following is given by the author as an assumption entertained by consumers?","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"A single factor cannot wholly explain the in-store variety and quality in American grocery stores.","B":"Advancement of technology has no significant effect on the business of grocery stores in America.","C":"The seasonality of agricultural produce has been overcome by advancement of food science.","D":"Fragmentation of the grocery business has not been favorable to it in the long run."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"The seasonality of agricultural produce has been overcome by advancement of food science.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"confuses_inference_with_assumption","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"The question asks for the specific assumption entertained by consumers. The author mentions it in the fourth sentence of the first paragraph by stating 'we take for granted the ability to get almost any fruit or vegetable year-round'. Naturally, the assumption is the year-round availability of agricultural produce because of advancement of food science. Choice A is incorrect because this is the author's assumption in the very first sentence, not consumers' assumption. Choice B is incorrect because this does not support the observed increase in grocery business because of technology. Choice C is CORRECT as per the reasons given above. Choice D is incorrect because this is a statement that argues against fragmentation, which in fact is said to benefit the business.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E1_013","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P5","passage":"Although the light of almost four centuries has been focused on \\"The Prince,\\" its problems are still debatable and interesting, because they are the eternal problems between the ruled and their rulers. Such as they are, its ethics are those of Machiavelli's contemporaries; yet they cannot be said to be out of date so long as the governments of Europe rely on material rather than on moral forces.\\n\\n\\"The Prince\\" is bestrewn with truths that can be proved at every turn. Men are still the dupes of their simplicity and greed. The cloak of religion still conceals the vices which Machiavelli laid bare in the character of the rulers of his time. Men will not look at things as they really are, but as they wish them to be. In politics there are no perfectly safe courses; prudence consists in choosing the least dangerous ones. Machiavelli reiterates that, although crimes may win an empire, they do not win glory. Necessary wars are just wars, and the arms of a nation are hallowed when it has no other recourse but to fight.\\n\\nIt is the cry of a far later day than Machiavelli's that government should be elevated into a living moral force, capable of inspiring the people with a just recognition of the fundamental principles of society; to this \\"high argument\\" \\"The Prince\\" contributes but little. Machiavelli always refused to write either of men or of governments otherwise than as he found them, and he writes with such skill and insight that his work is of abiding value. But what invests \\"The Prince\\" with more than a merely artistic or historical interest is the incontrovertible truth that it deals with the great principles which still guide nations and rulers in their relationship with each other and their neighbors.","question":"According to the passage:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"'The Prince' has very few assertions that can be validated.","B":"'The Prince' strongly supports the belief that government should be carried out on moral principles.","C":"'The Prince' details the attributes of ideal rulers.","D":"'The Prince' was written about 400 years ago."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"'The Prince' was written about 400 years ago.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"contradicts_passage","C":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"For this specific detail question, we need to verify each option individually. Choice A is incorrect because, as per the passage, the government runs on 'materialistic factors' and The Prince is full of truths. Choice B is incorrect because Machiavelli actually supports the view that material factors are more important than moral ones. Choice C is incorrect because the passage posits that Machiavelli does not endorse ideals. Choice D is CORRECT because the passage opens with 'the light of almost four centuries has been focused on The Prince', making it approximately 400 years old.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E1_014","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P5","passage":"Although the light of almost four centuries has been focused on \\"The Prince,\\" its problems are still debatable and interesting, because they are the eternal problems between the ruled and their rulers. Such as they are, its ethics are those of Machiavelli's contemporaries; yet they cannot be said to be out of date so long as the governments of Europe rely on material rather than on moral forces.\\n\\n\\"The Prince\\" is bestrewn with truths that can be proved at every turn. Men are still the dupes of their simplicity and greed. The cloak of religion still conceals the vices which Machiavelli laid bare in the character of the rulers of his time. Men will not look at things as they really are, but as they wish them to be. In politics there are no perfectly safe courses; prudence consists in choosing the least dangerous ones. Machiavelli reiterates that, although crimes may win an empire, they do not win glory. Necessary wars are just wars, and the arms of a nation are hallowed when it has no other recourse but to fight.\\n\\nIt is the cry of a far later day than Machiavelli's that government should be elevated into a living moral force, capable of inspiring the people with a just recognition of the fundamental principles of society; to this \\"high argument\\" \\"The Prince\\" contributes but little. Machiavelli always refused to write either of men or of governments otherwise than as he found them, and he writes with such skill and insight that his work is of abiding value. But what invests \\"The Prince\\" with more than a merely artistic or historical interest is the incontrovertible truth that it deals with the great principles which still guide nations and rulers in their relationship with each other and their neighbors.","question":"The problems spoken of in 'The Prince'","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"are those of temporary nature.","B":"are no longer relevant.","C":"are those that arise between a government and its citizens.","D":"are not those that can be discussed in the context of the present day."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"are those that arise between a government and its citizens.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","B":"contradicts_passage","D":"contradicts_passage"},"explanation":"For this question, we need to verify each option individually. Choice A is incorrect because the author explicitly mentions that these problems are relevant even today — not temporary. Choice B is incorrect for the same reason. Choice C is CORRECT because of the first two sentences of the first paragraph, which state these are the eternal problems between the ruled and their rulers. Choice D is incorrect because the problems are explicitly stated to be relevant even today.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E1_015","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P5","passage":"Although the light of almost four centuries has been focused on \\"The Prince,\\" its problems are still debatable and interesting, because they are the eternal problems between the ruled and their rulers. Such as they are, its ethics are those of Machiavelli's contemporaries; yet they cannot be said to be out of date so long as the governments of Europe rely on material rather than on moral forces.\\n\\n\\"The Prince\\" is bestrewn with truths that can be proved at every turn. Men are still the dupes of their simplicity and greed. The cloak of religion still conceals the vices which Machiavelli laid bare in the character of the rulers of his time. Men will not look at things as they really are, but as they wish them to be. In politics there are no perfectly safe courses; prudence consists in choosing the least dangerous ones. Machiavelli reiterates that, although crimes may win an empire, they do not win glory. Necessary wars are just wars, and the arms of a nation are hallowed when it has no other recourse but to fight.\\n\\nIt is the cry of a far later day than Machiavelli's that government should be elevated into a living moral force, capable of inspiring the people with a just recognition of the fundamental principles of society; to this \\"high argument\\" \\"The Prince\\" contributes but little. Machiavelli always refused to write either of men or of governments otherwise than as he found them, and he writes with such skill and insight that his work is of abiding value. But what invests \\"The Prince\\" with more than a merely artistic or historical interest is the incontrovertible truth that it deals with the great principles which still guide nations and rulers in their relationship with each other and their neighbors.","question":"According to the author, Machiavelli believes all of the following to be true, EXCEPT:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"crimes sometimes bring about honorable results.","B":"crimes may help establish dominion.","C":"war is justified when circumstances require it.","D":"war is justified when a nation has no other way of securing its interests."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"crimes sometimes bring about honorable results.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"premise_restatement","C":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"For this EXCEPT question, we need to verify each option individually and eliminate all that are true according to the passage. Choice A is CORRECT because this is not a true statement — the author explicitly states that 'crimes do not bring glory', directly contradicting the idea that crimes bring honorable results. Choice B is incorrect because this is a true statement, inferable from the last two sentences of the second paragraph. Choice C is incorrect because this is also a true statement, inferable from the last sentence of the second paragraph. Choice D is incorrect because this statement can be inferred from the last sentence of the second paragraph.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E1_016","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P6","passage":"The origin of Earth's water is an unsolved mystery, solving which may be a key to assessing the evolutionary history and modern structure of the Earth and has implications for how much water Mars and other planets accreted. The grand topic also bears fundamental importance to astro-biology.\\n\\nA successful theory of how the Earth acquired its water must explain both the amount and isotopic composition of hydrogen on the Earth's surface and its mantle. Because hydrogen is easily oxidized to form water, the origin of the Earth's water is equivalent to the sources of its hydrogen. In addition to one ocean of water on the Earth's surface, another several oceans' worth of hydrogen as OH and H2O are believed to reside in the Earth's mantle.\\n\\nThe current consensus is that the Earth acquired most of its water by accretion of carbonaceous chondrite material from beyond the snow line in the solar nebula. Many scholars find it likely in their simulations of planetary accretion that the Earth could have accreted a single planetary embryo which would have provided the Earth with many tens of oceans of water. This consensus is bolstered by the observation that structurally bound water in carbonaceous chondrites, on average, has isotopic composition of hydrogen similar to that of the terrestrial water.\\n\\nThis consensus view implicitly makes two assumptions, which may or may not be justified. First, most models discount the contributions to the Earth's hydrogen from in gassing of nebular H2 and H2O. But in fact, it has long been recognized from noble gas isotopic ratios that the Earth's interior contains some He and Ne contributed from the solar nebula. Second, in noting the isotopic match between chondrites and the Earth, the consensus view presumes that the hydrogen in the Earth's mantle and surface reflects the entirety of its hydrogen. In fact, the Earth's core must contain a significant fraction of light elements such as silicon, oxygen and sulfur. This inventory may include hydrogen, although first-principles molecular dynamics calculations yield opposing and controversial conclusions about the amount of hydrogen that can match seismic data.","question":"The passage is primarily concerned with","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"solving a mystery about the origin of water.","B":"calling into question a contemporary hypothesis.","C":"qualifying a current hypothesis.","D":"explaining assumptions behind a current hypothesis."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"calling into question a contemporary hypothesis.","difficulty":"medium","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"out_of_scope","C":"correct_but_unstated","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"The passage introduces a mystery, presents a hypothesis (current consensus) and in the last paragraph brings out the questionable nature of the assumptions behind that theory. Thus, overall, it tries to question a theory explaining the origin of water. Choice A is incorrect because the passage does not solve the problem. Choice B is CORRECT as per the reasons given above. Choice C is incorrect because the author does not qualify — put conditions on — the current hypothesis. Choice D is incorrect because the author does not explain but questions the assumptions.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E1_017","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P6","passage":"The origin of Earth's water is an unsolved mystery, solving which may be a key to assessing the evolutionary history and modern structure of the Earth and has implications for how much water Mars and other planets accreted. The grand topic also bears fundamental importance to astro-biology.\\n\\nA successful theory of how the Earth acquired its water must explain both the amount and isotopic composition of hydrogen on the Earth's surface and its mantle. Because hydrogen is easily oxidized to form water, the origin of the Earth's water is equivalent to the sources of its hydrogen. In addition to one ocean of water on the Earth's surface, another several oceans' worth of hydrogen as OH and H2O are believed to reside in the Earth's mantle.\\n\\nThe current consensus is that the Earth acquired most of its water by accretion of carbonaceous chondrite material from beyond the snow line in the solar nebula. Many scholars find it likely in their simulations of planetary accretion that the Earth could have accreted a single planetary embryo which would have provided the Earth with many tens of oceans of water. This consensus is bolstered by the observation that structurally bound water in carbonaceous chondrites, on average, has isotopic composition of hydrogen similar to that of the terrestrial water.\\n\\nThis consensus view implicitly makes two assumptions, which may or may not be justified. First, most models discount the contributions to the Earth's hydrogen from in gassing of nebular H2 and H2O. But in fact, it has long been recognized from noble gas isotopic ratios that the Earth's interior contains some He and Ne contributed from the solar nebula. Second, in noting the isotopic match between chondrites and the Earth, the consensus view presumes that the hydrogen in the Earth's mantle and surface reflects the entirety of its hydrogen. In fact, the Earth's core must contain a significant fraction of light elements such as silicon, oxygen and sulfur. This inventory may include hydrogen, although first-principles molecular dynamics calculations yield opposing and controversial conclusions about the amount of hydrogen that can match seismic data.","question":"The author's tone towards current consensus is","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"enthusiastic.","B":"critical.","C":"ambivalent.","D":"sarcastic."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"critical.","difficulty":"easy","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","C":"correct_but_unstated","D":"too_extreme"},"explanation":"The author introduces the topic of the mystery of the origin of water on Earth, presents what an ideal theory should be and questions the current consensus by pointing out the flaws in the consensus view. Thus, his tone is primarily critical. Choice A is incorrect because the author does not praise the theory. Choice B is CORRECT as per the reasons given above. Choice C is incorrect because he is not 'undecided' about the validity of the theory — he actually criticizes it. Choice D is incorrect because his tone is not 'to make fun' of the theory.","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"RC_E1_018","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P6","passage":"The origin of Earth's water is an unsolved mystery, solving which may be a key to assessing the evolutionary history and modern structure of the Earth and has implications for how much water Mars and other planets accreted. The grand topic also bears fundamental importance to astro-biology.\\n\\nA successful theory of how the Earth acquired its water must explain both the amount and isotopic composition of hydrogen on the Earth's surface and its mantle. Because hydrogen is easily oxidized to form water, the origin of the Earth's water is equivalent to the sources of its hydrogen. In addition to one ocean of water on the Earth's surface, another several oceans' worth of hydrogen as OH and H2O are believed to reside in the Earth's mantle.\\n\\nThe current consensus is that the Earth acquired most of its water by accretion of carbonaceous chondrite material from beyond the snow line in the solar nebula. Many scholars find it likely in their simulations of planetary accretion that the Earth could have accreted a single planetary embryo which would have provided the Earth with many tens of oceans of water. This consensus is bolstered by the observation that structurally bound water in carbonaceous chondrites, on average, has isotopic composition of hydrogen similar to that of the terrestrial water.\\n\\nThis consensus view implicitly makes two assumptions, which may or may not be justified. First, most models discount the contributions to the Earth's hydrogen from in gassing of nebular H2 and H2O. But in fact, it has long been recognized from noble gas isotopic ratios that the Earth's interior contains some He and Ne contained from the solar nebula. Second, in noting the isotopic match between chondrites and the Earth, the consensus view presumes that the hydrogen in the Earth's mantle and surface reflects the entirety of its hydrogen. In fact, the Earth's core must contain a significant fraction of light elements such as silicon, oxygen and sulfur. This inventory may include hydrogen, although first-principles molecular dynamics calculations yield opposing and controversial conclusions about the amount of hydrogen that can match seismic data.","question":"According to the passage, solving the mystery mentioned in the first paragraph will help in understanding all of the following EXCEPT:","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"the sources of Earth's hydrogen.","B":"the isotopic composition of hydrogen.","C":"some aspects of astro-biology.","D":"geological evolution."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"the isotopic composition of hydrogen.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"premise_restatement","C":"premise_restatement","D":"premise_restatement"},"explanation":"For this kind of question, we need to verify each option individually and eliminate the correct options. Choice A is incorrect because this is something we can learn from solving the mystery, as per the second paragraph. Choice B is CORRECT because isotopic composition of hydrogen is mentioned as the evidence used to know the source of water, not as a benefit of solving the mystery — it is a tool, not an outcome. Choice C is incorrect because this information is given in the last sentence of the first paragraph. Choice D is incorrect because this is directly mentioned in the first paragraph.","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"RC_E1_019","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P6","passage":"The origin of Earth's water is an unsolved mystery, solving which may be a key to assessing the evolutionary history and modern structure of the Earth and has implications for how much water Mars and other planets accreted. The grand topic also bears fundamental importance to astro-biology.\\n\\nA successful theory of how the Earth acquired its water must explain both the amount and isotopic composition of hydrogen on the Earth's surface and its mantle. Because hydrogen is easily oxidized to form water, the origin of the Earth's water is equivalent to the sources of its hydrogen. In addition to one ocean of water on the Earth's surface, another several oceans' worth of hydrogen as OH and H2O are believed to reside in the Earth's mantle.\\n\\nThe current consensus is that the Earth acquired most of its water by accretion of carbonaceous chondrite material from beyond the snow line in the solar nebula. Many scholars find it likely in their simulations of planetary accretion that the Earth could have accreted a single planetary embryo which would have provided the Earth with many tens of oceans of water. This consensus is bolstered by the observation that structurally bound water in carbonaceous chondrites, on average, has isotopic composition of hydrogen similar to that of the terrestrial water.\\n\\nThis consensus view implicitly makes two assumptions, which may or may not be justified. First, most models discount the contributions to the Earth's hydrogen from in gassing of nebular H2 and H2O. But in fact, it has long been recognized from noble gas isotopic ratios that the Earth's interior contains some He and Ne contributed from the solar nebula. Second, in noting the isotopic match between chondrites and the Earth, the consensus view presumes that the hydrogen in the Earth's mantle and surface reflects the entirety of its hydrogen. In fact, the Earth's core must contain a significant fraction of light elements such as silicon, oxygen and sulfur. This inventory may include hydrogen, although first-principles molecular dynamics calculations yield opposing and controversial conclusions about the amount of hydrogen that can match seismic data.","question":"The author mentions noble gas isotopic ratios of Earth's interior to suggest the view that","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"the contributions of Earth's hydrogen from in gassing of nebular hydrogen and water have been underestimated by models supporting the consensus view.","B":"nebular in gassing of hydrogen and water contributed little to the hydrogen present in the Earth's atmosphere and the Earth's mantle.","C":"structurally bound water in carbonaceous chondrites has isotopic composition of hydrogen isotopes on the Earth.","D":"the assumption on which the consensus view is based is correct only in some cases."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"the contributions of Earth's hydrogen from in gassing of nebular hydrogen and water have been underestimated by models supporting the consensus view.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"B":"contradicts_passage","C":"out_of_scope","D":"correct_but_unstated"},"explanation":"The author mentions most models' discounting of the contributions from nebular hydrogen and water. Then he uses 'but' and mentions the isotopic ratio and suggests that the gases were from the solar nebula. This implies that his mentioning of the isotopic ratio is to prove that the earlier models' discounting is wrong. Choice A is CORRECT as per the reasons given above. Choice B is incorrect because this is what the author disproves by using the isotopic ratios. Choice C is incorrect because this is not related to the isotopic ratios mentioned in this paragraph. Choice D is incorrect because he uses this to question that assumption, not partially support it.","time_benchmark_seconds":105},{"id":"RC_E1_020","exercise":"exercise_1","passage_id":"RC_E1_P6","passage":"The origin of Earth's water is an unsolved mystery, solving which may be a key to assessing the evolutionary history and modern structure of the Earth and has implications for how much water Mars and other planets accreted. The grand topic also bears fundamental importance to astro-biology.\\n\\nA successful theory of how the Earth acquired its water must explain both the amount and isotopic composition of hydrogen on the Earth's surface and its mantle. Because hydrogen is easily oxidized to form water, the origin of the Earth's water is equivalent to the sources of its hydrogen. In addition to one ocean of water on the Earth's surface, another several oceans' worth of hydrogen as OH and H2O are believed to reside in the Earth's mantle.\\n\\nThe current consensus is that the Earth acquired most of its water by accretion of carbonaceous chondrite material from beyond the snow line in the solar nebula. Many scholars find it likely in their simulations of planetary accretion that the Earth could have accreted a single planetary embryo which would have provided the Earth with many tens of oceans of water. This consensus is bolstered by the observation that structurally bound water in carbonaceous chondrites, on average, has isotopic composition of hydrogen similar to that of the terrestrial water.\\n\\nThis consensus view implicitly makes two assumptions, which may or may not be justified. First, most models discount the contributions to the Earth's hydrogen from in gassing of nebular H2 and H2O. But in fact, it has long been recognized from noble gas isotopic ratios that the Earth's interior contains some He and Ne contributed from the solar nebula. Second, in noting the isotopic match between chondrites and the Earth, the consensus view presumes that the hydrogen in the Earth's mantle and surface reflects the entirety of its hydrogen. In fact, the Earth's core must contain a significant fraction of light elements such as silicon, oxygen and sulfur. This inventory may include hydrogen, although first-principles molecular dynamics calculations yield opposing and controversial conclusions about the amount of hydrogen that can match seismic data.","question":"The author mentions first-principles molecular dynamics calculations yielding opposing and controversial conclusions in order to","type":"MCQ","options":{"A":"add further evidence in support of his criticism.","B":"state a point that may work against his criticism.","C":"bring out the difference between the two assumptions he attacks.","D":"sum up the argument presented all through the passage."},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"state a point that may work against his criticism.","difficulty":"hard","topic":"Reading Comprehension","common_errors":{"A":"contradicts_passage","C":"out_of_scope","D":"out_of_scope"},"explanation":"The author criticizes the second assumption, and states a view (the inventory may include hydrogen) that attacks the assumption. Then the author presents a fact (by using 'although') that may be against his criticism — the molecular dynamics calculations yield opposing conclusions about hydrogen in the core. Thus, Option B, which states this, is the best option. Choice A is incorrect because the calculations actually weaken the author's criticism. Choice B is CORRECT as per the reasons given above. Choice C is incorrect because the author does not mention the calculations to contrast the two assumptions. Choice D is incorrect as this does not sum up.","time_benchmark_seconds":105}]`);export{e as default};
